  "response": "The question of whether democratic institutions themselves account for the absence of war between democracies, or whether that pattern merely reflects correlated features of the states involved, has been addressed primarily through quantitative designs that permit the simultaneous estimation of competing influences. Political scientists commonly test the democratic peace using dyadic statistical analyses of pairs of states, since the hypothesis concerns how two regimes behave toward each other rather than how a single regime behaves in general. For example, Oneal and Russett used the dyad-year as their unit of analysis; that is, each observation in Oneal and Russett's dataset was a pair of states in a given year. This structure allows researchers to code the regime type of both partners, the volume of commerce between them, their alliance ties, and their relative capabilities for every year in which conflict could have occurred, so that rival explanations can enter the same model.\n\nThe most comprehensive application of this design appeared in book form. Bruce Russett and John Oneal co-authored the 2001 book \"Triangulating Peace,\" and because they undertook that collaborative volume, Russett and Oneal included bilateral trade dependence as a control variable alongside joint democracy. The inclusion of commercial ties was meant to establish whether the apparent effect of regime type was in fact an artifact of the dense economic exchange that tends to connect democratic states. By contrast, the \"Triangulating Peace\" analysis concluded that joint democracy retained an independent pacifying effect after controlling for trade, alliances, and relative power. The book's title refers to this triangulation of democracy, interdependence, and international organization as mutually reinforcing but separately measurable sources of restraint.\n\nEstablishing that joint democracy matters statistically does not by itself identify the mechanism through which it operates, and scholars have pursued this second question with comparable care. Two accounts dominate the literature: at least one of the following holds, perhaps both, namely that shared democratic norms of peaceful compromise restrain conflict between democracies, or that institutional constraints on democratic leaders restrain conflict between democracies. The normative account emphasizes the externalization of domestic bargaining practices, whereas the structural account stresses electoral accountability, legislative oversight, and the slow mobilization of consent. Zeev Maoz and Bruce Russett compared normative and structural explanations of the democratic peace in a 1993 American Political Science Review article, operationalizing each mechanism with distinct indicators. The theoretical lineage of these debates extends further back: Michael Doyle linked the separate peace among liberal states to Immanuel Kant's ideas in a 1983 essay, after he first published his liberal peace thesis in 1975.\n\nThe most serious recent challenge to the institutional interpretation comes from economic theory. Erik Gartzke advanced a \"capitalist peace\" argument emphasizing financial openness and market development, as confirmed by his publication of \"The Capitalist Peace\" in the journal International Organization. On this view, developed market economies have fewer incentives to seize territory and more capacity to signal resolve through capital flows, so that the pacifying influence attributed to democracy is really a product of economic liberalism. Although Gartzke advanced that argument, the empirical record of his own specifications is contested, and the dispute takes an exclusive form: either in Gartzke's capitalist peace models the coefficient on joint democracy remained statistically significant after controls for economic development and financial openness were added, or in Gartzke's capitalist peace models the coefficient on joint democracy lost statistical significance after controls for economic development and financial openness were added. The two readings cannot both be true, and one of them must hold, which is why replication became the decisive test.\n\nThat replication effort settled the matter. In contrast to the capitalist peace claim that markets rather than institutions do the pacifying work, Dafoe, Oneal, and Russett ultimately concluded in a 2013 International Studies Quarterly article that the democratic peace remains robust when tested against capitalist peace specifications. Their reanalysis resolves the tension between Gartzke's theoretical challenge and the persistence of the democracy coefficient, and it illustrates the broader methodological lesson of this research program: confounding explanations are best adjudicated not by argument alone but by placing them in the same dyadic model, examining how sensitive each estimate is to measurement and specification choices, and asking which effects survive.",
